<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Antonius_Guilielmus_Amo_Dissertation_1734.jpg/960px-Antonius_Guilielmus_Amo_Dissertation_1734.jpg" alt="Title page of Anton Wilhelm Amo's 1734 doctoral dissertation at Wittenberg, printed in Latin" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-8" loading="lazy" width="960" height="1299" />

<p>In 1734, at the University of Wittenberg, a young man defended a Latin dissertation on whether the human mind can feel. Public disputations were the examination system of the early Enlightenment: you stood in front of the faculty, you printed your thesis, and you argued for it in a language that had not been anyone's mother tongue for a thousand years. The candidate's name on the title page was <em>Antonius Guilielmus Amo Afer</em> - Anton Wilhelm Amo, the African.</p>

<p>He had been born around 1703 in Axim, on the coast of what is now Ghana, among the Nzema, an Akan people. He had been in Europe since he was about four. Everything he knew about law, logic, medicine and metaphysics he had learned in Latin and German, in institutions that had never before awarded a doctorate to a man from sub-Saharan Africa. And the first thing he had chosen to argue in public, five years earlier, was that Africans in Europe had rights.</p>

<p>The sheet itself survives, and it is the image at the top of this page. It was printed at Wittenberg in April 1734 by the Schlomach press, and it announces the defense in the larger auditorium: the author, <em>Antonius Guilielmus Amo, Guinea-Afer, Philosophiae et Artium Liberalium Magister</em>, defending a thesis on the absence of sensation in the human mind, under the presidency of Martin Gotthelf Löscher, professor of medicine and physics and provincial physician to the Duke of Saxe-Weimar. Guinea-Afer. He put the place he came from onto the title page of his own book, in Latin, in capitals, when he could have left it off.</p>

